# Cursor  
#  Cursor is an AI-powered code editor.`.cursorignore` specifies files/directories to 
#  exclude from AI features like autocomplete and code analysis. Recommended for sensitive data
#  refer to https://docs.cursor.com/context/ignore-files
.cursorignore
.cursorindexingignore
.DS_Store

# Excel lock files from opening the planning manifest
~$*.xlsx

# Raw and processed genomics data: FASTQs, genomes, STAR indices, bigWigs,
# peak calls. ~120 GiB of FASTQ alone for the full experiment set.
# Anchored with a leading slash, matching /qc/ below. An UNANCHORED `modisco/`
# matched src/bpnet/modisco/ too and silently untracked the tfmodisco launchers
# -- git added nothing and reported nothing. Only `modisco` and `qc` actually
# collide with a source directory today, but anchoring the whole block costs
# nothing and stops the next one.
/data/
/models/
/logs/
/predictions/
/performance_metrics/
/plots/
/attributions/
/modisco/
/data/annotation/

# QC output, including the stats tables. These are RUN ARTIFACTS and are
# deliberately not tracked: qc/stats/experiment_stats.{tsv,md} are also outputs
# of the stats_table rule, so a committed copy makes Snakemake report "Nothing
# to be done" and never rebuild it -- a stale table that looks authoritative is
# worse than no table. Read them where the pipeline ran, or refresh locally with
#   python src/qc/experiment_stats.py --all -o qc/stats/experiment_stats.tsv \
#       --markdown qc/stats/experiment_stats.md
#
# ANCHORED with a leading slash on purpose. A bare `qc/` matches a directory of
# that name at ANY depth, so it also ignored **src/qc/** -- the QC *source*
# tree. read_structure_qc.py was silently skipped by `git add -A` because of it,
# and orientation_qc.py / experiment_stats.py are tracked only because they
# predate the rule. Do not remove the slash.
/qc/

# Snakemake working metadata
.snakemake/
